From 4a6644d3c53bc11bf852d46e7f4eb81e82873491 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 07:44:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Give a JSON module the ES namespace ESM wants and require the
 value CommonJS wants
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`import d from './data.json'` was `undefined`, which made JSON modules
unusable. One wrapper served both callers — `module.exports = <json>` — which
is exactly what CommonJS require wants and exactly wrong for ESM: a default
import reads `.default` off the parsed value and finds nothing. Every JSON
import was undefined, for every JSON shape. A file whose whole content is
`null` was worse: JSModule's Exports setter refuses null, so it threw on load
rather than importing as null.

The two specifications disagree about what a JSON file exports and they cannot
be satisfied by one object. ES2025 gives a JSON module exactly one export,
`default`, holding the parsed value, and no named exports; CommonJS
require('./x.json') hands back the parsed value itself. So the module now
STORES the ES namespace — `module.exports = { default: (<json>) }` — and the
CommonJS view unwraps it. LoadModuleAsync takes an `esModule` flag for that,
defaulting to the ES view, and the two require call sites pass false; the flag
changes nothing except the shape a JSON module is presented in.

Storing the namespace is also what lets `null` through, and what makes arrays,
numbers, strings and booleans work: none of them can carry a `default`
property, so no amount of decorating the parsed value could have fixed them.

Deliberate deviation, now pinned rather than incidental: `import { a } from
'./x.json'` used to read `a` off the parsed object and is now undefined. Per
spec it is a link error — a JSON module has no named exports — and raising that
needs whole-module link analysis this engine does not do, so the nearer of the
two available answers is taken. Browsers and Node both reject the form.

Fourteen regressions in JsonModuleTests: the default import over six JSON
shapes, the namespace holding only `default`, a dynamic import resolving to the
same namespace, require over three shapes, both views of one file agreeing on
the value, the named-import deviation, and a JavaScript module pinned as
unaffected. Eleven of the fourteen fail before this change.

Engine suites: modules 118/118, module-extensions 5/5, built-ins 2215/2215,
integration 5167/5168, compiler 1400/1402 — the three failures pre-existing and
unrelated. Broiler.Cli.Tests module/subdocument/import selection 322/323, its
one failure the known iframe-module drain gap, which reproduces with this
change stashed.
